Both runs loaded Linux, validated exact unique nonce markers, and reached
retired=2,999,999,998. Sidecars are schema v7, retained every selected-site
hit, and report zero omissions: 43,883 samples in run 0001 and 43,892 in run
0002. Each sidecar records 33 delivered #PF events. The series outcome is
`passed` for the smoke scenario, but the comparison is `matches=false` with
unexplained `machine_observations` and nonce-normalized `ordered_uart_bytes`.
The run profile is `smoke_not_acceptance`; acceptance is
`diagnostic_not_counted`.

Entry-sequence analysis
----------------------
Across all 43,883 shared hit ordinals, `requested_cycles`, RBP, and RSP are
identical in order. Run 0002 has nine additional samples at the tail; each
has requested_cycles `0xf4217` and RBP/RSP `0xffffc9000000fdf8`. This pair
therefore shows no request or stack-sequence difference among the shared
entry hits. It does not establish why run 0002 has additional tail hits.

CR3 differs from ordinal 10,022 onward in the shared sequence: `0x6484000`
in run 0001 and `0x648e000` in run 0002. The stack and request sequence still
matches at those ordinals. This records different physical page-table roots;
the cause and relevance are not established, and the difference is not
classified as benign.

The TSC value at each entry is an absolute counter sample. Comparing
consecutive-entry increments (current observed_tsc minus prior observed_tsc)
removes the starting-counter offset: 4,815 of 43,882 shared increments
differ. The first is the interval ending at hit ordinal 2: `0x53b4` in run
0001 versus `0x6ac4` in run 0002, while request `0x7d0` and RBP/RSP match.
These are between-entry increments, not elapsed cycles for an individual
delay_tsc call. They quantify variation but do not identify its cause.

At the selected entry RIP, RDI still contains the input cycle count, so the
sidecar's derived `start_tsc` and `elapsed_cycles` fields are not delay-loop
elapsed measurements. `requested_cycles` (R8), `observed_tsc` (EDX:EAX), and
the stack registers are the relevant fields here.

This entry-site pair has a different unique-nonce pair from the prior
stride-512 poll-site series; it cannot be joined hit-for-hit to that series.
Both series remain diagnostic and do not explain the strict machine/UART
differences or satisfy the counted S5-07 acceptance gate. A next observer
would need to tag poll samples with the corresponding delay_tsc entry-call
ordinal to compare poll counts per invocation.

